imisha Joyce wall  joins us now from Delhi Nimisha a good  to have you with us now our people in  the region worried that this conflict  could escalate into something more  dangerous I mean there are definitely  concerns because we are talking about  two massive nuclear-armed nations but in  India especially there is a difference  between what happens when we are in one  fixed with Pakistan versus when India  sees a conflict with China now the kind  of aggressive almost jingoism that we  see and assuring that we see with  Pakistan is largely absent when it comes  to China  analysts believe analysts believe that  this could be because of China's  conventional warfare military  superiority but on the other hand we  also see Indians coming together to try  to clamp down on trade ties with China  for example a removed China AB became  very popular in India in the last two  weeks because of the fact that they were  trying to remove all Chinese apps from  their phones they've also been Indian  celebrities coming out and saying stop  buying Chinese products hit them where  it hurts but of course there's a 90  billion dollar trade between the two  countries and downloading and removing a  few apps is probably not going to have  much of a damaging effect on the Asian  giant India and China seem to be making  an effort to resolve this conflict  military commanders are meeting at a  border post there now what are they  hoping to achieve well that's right I  mean already there have been ten rounds  of military talks at lower levels  leading up to this however this did not  lead to any positive results yesterday  there was some progress because  diplomats from the two countries got on  to a video conference together and spoke  about how a dispute between the two  countries must be resolved through  peaceful discussion these military talks  are a step towards that they are also  saying they do not expect a complete  resolution right now but it seems that  India has already stocks and India is  requesting for China to allow for  infrastructure development on the Indian  side India also demands that the troops  be withdrawn to the status who before  the tensions rose  which is exactly what happened when the  dockland standoff  20:17 ended what is behind these  repeated standoffs this isn't the first  time this has happened so what is  causing these well I mean this is  basically because the line between the  two countries the line of actual control  is a contested border there are large  swaths of with which the two countries  see eye to eye on but there are still  many points of conflict whether have  been advances and then have been  standoff similar to what we saw earlier  earlier last month in mail now one of  the main reasons that leads to these  standoffs is infrastructure development  on both sides we talked about how in  Dhokla in 2017 India try to stop  infrastructure development by China  there is also speculation that this  year's standoff over the last few weeks  has been because China has objected to  infrastructure development by India I'm  not the common allegation from both  sides is the troops have advanced into  the other territory this is what India  has said about Chinese troops this year  as well but again there is also concerns  and analyst surveys that because of  China's conventional military  superiority and because India would not  want to escalate this conflict further  this might be a way for China to take up  small sorts of land and call and create  land grabs so this is something that  India will definitely be watching for  and bringing up in discussions with  China mr Joyce wall in Delhi there thank  you
 

